add('''<figcaption>Source: final team sheet and current pyramid reference tables. The star-focus reference is a generated role-specialist value, not a universal opponent shooting rating.</figcaption></figure></div>
<div class="rule"><h3>The income and training loop compounded strongly</h3><div class="flow" aria-label="Observed growth loop"><span>Stands and fans</span><span>More cash</span><span>Pitch, staff, recruits</span><span>More useful stats</span><span>Wins and more fans</span></div><p>This loop is the game’s foundation, and it should stay powerful. It created satisfying decisions in D3 and D2. The question is when the remaining purchases and opponents stop putting pressure on it.</p><p>By late D1, the club could afford requests that would have threatened early payroll. I did not exhaust all available facility upgrades. That means the report supports testing the value of existing sinks, not claiming that no sinks exist.</p></div>
<div class="rule"><h3>Cheap academy development is good; instant flipping is not</h3><p>Cal cost $500 and became a cup-winning hero over six seasons. That is one of the strongest stories the game produced. Dara’s immediate $500-to-$6,052 sale used the same intake as a cash transaction with no development.</p><p><strong>Fix the resale timing or fee calculation. Keep the possibility of finding a cheap future star.</strong> An across-the-board academy price increase would damage the better story while addressing the wrong part of the exploit.</p></div>
<div class="rule"><h3>The scout ceiling can be legal and still too generous</h3><p>Jae’s OVR 86 passed the current D5 ceiling of 88. The source derives that ceiling from the next division’s support and star-focus ratings. This was not evidence of a bypass. It was evidence that a legal, affordable specialist can sharply outclass the current role options.</p><p>Before reducing every scout, compare the best available role upgrade, the transfer fee, the signed wage, and the promises required. Keep rare exciting finds. Reduce routine bargain dominance if broader samples confirm it.</p></div>
<div class="rule"><h3>Energy choices need more than a nominal downside</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Energy mode</th><th>Drain multiplier</th><th>Movement multiplier</th><th>What happened in this run</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Save Energy</td><td>0.60×</td><td>0.90×</td><td>The usual opening choice; often kept for 90 minutes.</td></tr><tr><td>Balanced</td><td>1.00×</td><td>1.00×</td><td>Used less once the energy tradeoff was understood.</td></tr><tr><td>All Out</td><td>1.65×</td><td>1.12×</td><td>Useful as a late chase, including the Silverbeck comeback.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The movement penalty has context and carrier exceptions. These numbers alone do not measure possession or chance creation. Test identical matches before nerfing Save Energy; the late All Out decision was one of the good parts of the run.</p></div>
<div class="rule"><h3>Higher drill tiers are not always better per TP</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Preview</th><th>Base gain</th><th>TP cost</th><th>Base gain / TP</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tier 4</td><td>13</td><td>26</td><td>0.500</td></tr><tr><td>Tier 5</td><td>18</td><td>39</td><td>0.462</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Tier 5 gives more per activation but about 7.7% less base gain per TP. Bonuses and SUPER rolls change actual gains. Keeping Tier 4 was a defensible resource choice, not evidence that Tier 5 failed to work.</p></div>
<div class="rule"><h3>Licenses and promises need one coherent explanation</h3><p>Three separate rules interacted: unlicensed heroes could not play; full capacity blocked available promised starters from awakening; and later seasons allowed at most one ordinary awakening. The SUPER percentage shown on a player is the drill’s 1.5× gain chance, not their awakening chance.</p><p>The license purchase was meaningful. Having to read code to understand whether it could unlock progress was not. Show the current block in the permit office and at contract signing. Keep the annual pacing rule visible without revealing every random roll.</p></div>
<div class="rule"><h3>Protect is not proven broken</h3><p>The first D1 Yewtree match fell from 3–1 to 3–3 after a defensive retreat. That is a reason to investigate outlets and pressure, not proof that Protect causes late concessions. Other earlier wins used it successfully.</p><p>Do not buff Protect solely to validate my expectation that a lead should be safe. Compare similar matches and inspect the chances created by both teams.</p></div></section>
<section id="fun"><h2>What was fun, and what was not</h2><p class="lead">The game’s strongest moments connected a player, a long-term decision, and a result I could recognize.</p><div class="columns" style="margin-top:28px"><div class="good"><h3>Keep these sources of satisfaction</h3><ul><li><strong>Players with a history.</strong> Ace, Cal and Sam remained relevant through the climb. Cal’s academy origin mattered at the final.</li><li><strong>Visible power identities.</strong> Phase Run, Blink Run, Shadow Mark, Super Strength and Ice Rink made heroes distinct from a higher overall number.</li><li><strong>Close rivals.</strong> Lumen, Peregrine, Garnet and Tarn gave individual seasons a shape.</li><li><strong>Useful rematches.</strong> A team that beat us could later be beaten through a better squad and plan.</li><li><strong>Recoverable failure.</strong> Missing promotion, taking a loan, and losing a cup tie did not end the career.</li><li><strong>The authored ending.</strong> The final screen acknowledged both trophies and the relationship with Ace. It gave the run a proper stopping point.</li></ul></div><div class="friction"><h3>Reduce these sources of fatigue</h3><ul><li><strong>Training correction.</strong> A confirmed batch already finishes when its presentation is dismissed. Cross-player setup is normal management, not a proven defect.</li><li><strong>Uncertain clicks.</strong> A hidden presentation layer sometimes made the next action feel ignored.</li><li><strong>Long hopeless matches.</strong> After a huge cup gap became clear, few useful decisions remained.</li><li><strong>Invisible dependencies.</strong> Permits and promises affected awakening without a clear combined explanation.</li><li><strong>Repeated low-value choices.</strong> A reserve’s personal training penalty often made the request an easy yes.</li><li><strong>Late routine wins.</strong> The final cup was a reward sequence more than a competitive test.</li></ul></div></div>
<div class="chart-card"><h3>The engagement arc, as an editorial reading</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Season</th><th>Best source of tension</th><th>Where it weakened</th><th>Overall read</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>S1</td><td>Payroll and recovery from early losses</td><td>Tutorial pace; late scouting by me</td><td>Slow start; rewarding improvement</td></tr><tr><td>S2</td><td>Final-day Lumen title race</td><td>Routine league routs; punishing fire</td><td>Big highs with uneven resistance</td></tr><tr><td>S3</td><td>Peregrine and promotion pressure</td><td>Crashes and an unusable new hero</td><td>Good sporting tension, weak trust</td></tr><tr><td>S4</td><td>Rebuild choices and important rematches</td><td>Another early cup wall</td><td>Strong management pacing</td></tr><tr><td>S5</td><td>First credible deep cup run</td><td>Browser recovery and repeated setup</td><td>Best mix of progress and ambition</td></tr><tr><td>S6</td><td>Early D1 tests and a late winner</td><td>Easy cup run and late resource surplus</td><td>Strong payoff, soft finale</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="small">These labels are judgments, not recorded player sentiment scores.</p></div>
<h3>The two moments I would design around</h3><div class="columns"><div class="callout"><h3>The Lumen title decider</h3><p>Both teams could finish on 51 points. Winning 5–1 away turned a season of investment into a clear trophy moment. The opponent stayed relevant all season.</p></div><div class="callout"><h3>The Silverbeck comeback</h3><p>Down 0–2, level at 2–2, then an 89th-minute winner after Attack and late All Out. The controls had a reason to exist. The outcome was uncertain until the end.</p></div></div>
<h3>Presentation changes I would make carefully</h3><p>Keep a full first awakening and a clear first explanation of each power. Keep the existing drill skip, SUPER feedback, and injury checks. Any smaller repeat power presentation remains a design choice.</p><p>Do not solve control visibility by freezing every power. The better target is continuous access to pause and tactical actions, with smaller repeat overlays.</p></section>
<section id="recommendations"><h2>What I would fix first — and what I would leave alone</h2>
<div class="decision"><div class="decision-label">First</div><div><h3>Restore trust in actions and lineups</h3><p>Fix CanvasKit recovery, diagnose the browser crash, and make lineup repair role-safe. A player should never need to inspect a save or catch a forward silently assigned to defense.</p><p class="small">Findings 01–06. Success means correct persisted state, reliable input, and a valid visible XI after each transition.</p></div></div>
<div class="decision"><div class="decision-label">Next</div><div><h3>Explain the consequential rules before the player commits</h3><p>Unify permits, promises and awakening. Name the buildings at risk in destructive stories. Distinguish an estimated bid from an actual offer. Add the last useful scouting date.</p><p class="small">Findings 09, 14–18. This improves fairness without changing a single match stat.</p></div></div>
<div class="decision"><div class="decision-label">Then</div><div><h3>Keep the training flow; review power controls separately</h3><p>Training already previews batch spending and allows outside dismissal. No training behavior change is needed from this evidence. Decide separately whether power overlays should leave coaching controls visible.</p><p class="small">Findings 04, 25, 29. Fast automation amplified some delays; ordinary touch use was not measured.</p></div></div>
<div class="decision"><div class="decision-label">Test before tuning</div><div><h3>Address the growth curve and cup extremes</h3><p>Close academy flipping narrowly. Measure scout bargains, high-rating training, energy modes, cup margins and D1 resistance over multiple careers. Change the smallest mechanism supported by the results.</p><p class="small">Findings 19–24, 26–28. No blanket difficulty increase is justified by this one optimized run.</p></div></div>
<div class="decision keep"><div class="decision-label">Keep</div><div><h3>The long-term player story</h3><p>Do not force replacement of every starter on promotion. Cal’s six-season development and Sam’s continued usefulness created continuity. Keep that possibility while testing runaway specialist growth.</p></div></div>
<div class="decision keep"><div class="decision-label">Keep</div><div><h3>Promotion from second place and the fail-soft economy</h3><p>D4 rewarded the season even without its title. The loan allowed recovery. A player should be able to make a mistake and still have a believable next move.</p></div></div>
<div class="decision keep"><div class="decision-label">Keep</div><div><h3>Rare big wins, SUPER drills and useful powers</h3><p>A rout is not automatically a bug. A surprise training gain is enjoyable variance. The concern is repeated dominance erasing choices, not the existence of a spectacular result.</p></div></div>
<div class="decision keep"><div class="decision-label">Keep</div><div><h3>Quick Match and a real ending</h3><p>Quick Match made clear advantages efficient to play through. The explicit double-trophy ending gave the career closure. Do not add extra seasons merely to lengthen an already completed arc.</p></div></div>
<div class="callout"><h3>Changes I would avoid now</h3><p>Do not raise all wages, weaken every scout, delete Save Energy, add hidden catch-up scaling, or remove strong hero movement from this evidence alone. Do not add more surprise facility destruction as a late-money sink. Each risks damaging the best parts while leaving the actual trust and pacing issues intact.</p></div>
<h3>Small follow-up tests with high value</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Test</th><th>What would change my recommendation?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Is Save Energy too strong?</td><td>Same teams and seed across all three energy modes; compare chances and energy.</td><td>A clear context where Balanced or early All Out wins enough to justify its cost.</td></tr><tr><td>Are scouting bargains routine?</td><td>Sample each division and brief; compare total signing cost with role improvement.</td><td>Jae-like upgrades are rare, costly in other ways, or do not materially lift team outcomes.</td></tr><tr><td>Is the cup gap typical?</td><td>Log draw strength, division gap, upset rate, score margin, and useful decision time.</td><td>Most careers produce varied, close later rounds and this draw was exceptional.</td></tr><tr><td>Does training need a shortcut?</td><td>Ask new players to execute the same weekly plan with and without a preset.</td><td>The shortcut saves little time or makes spending and injury risk harder to understand.</td></tr><tr><td>Are permits understandable?</td><td>Ask players to predict what happens when a promised starter awakens at full capacity.</td><td>Players can predict the result and find the solution without outside help.</td></tr><tr><td>Does late money already have useful sinks?</td><td>Compare my targeted plan with full sensible facility and Green Bull investment.</td><td>Existing purchases remain compelling and their value is communicated clearly.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>For future playtests, record match mode, starting roles, permits, promises, condition, tactical inputs, shots, and cash changes alongside the result. That makes it possible to separate a better squad from a better tactic, and a presentation failure from a failed action.</p></section>
<section id="evidence"><h2>Evidence, limits, and corrections</h2><p>This report combines live UI results, the running notes, selected archived tool outputs, and targeted read-only source checks. It does not use old playtests as fresh results.</p>
<div class="evidence-block"><h3>Test conditions</h3><ul><li>One fresh Chairman career. One reset was explicitly authorized at the start.</li><li>Same build at the start and report check: <code>822e306a6a059837a5710d2a83049ed03d10d2bf</code>.</li><li>Built-in browser at localhost:8092. The ending remained visible and silent.</li><li>No later reset, match replay, save editing, or game-source change.</li><li>Read-only source inspection was used to understand rules. This gave the manager knowledge a normal player would not have.</li><li>The goal clock recorded 6h 26m. It includes automation, reading, recovery, and waiting. It is not a human completion-time benchmark.</li></ul></div>
<details class="evidence-block"><summary>Primary run notes and archived evidence</summary><p><code>artifacts/playtest-2026-09-07/notes.md</code> records the same career. Lines 236–305 cover S1–S3 and the S4 opening; 310–330 cover S4–S5; 332–340 cover S6 and the verified ending. Earlier lines contain the opening economy, UI warnings, and S1 results.</p><p>The current task’s archived tool log was used to recover the exact Ulric 12–0 full-time report and the fire choice’s actual wording. It also confirmed the Bruce Wain introduction. Private session logs are not bundled into this shareable report.</p><p>The report is self-contained. It does not need those files to render, and it does not include save data, credentials, or account details.</p></details>
<details class="evidence-block"><summary>Rules checked in the current source</summary><p><code>src/game/post-match-awakening.ts</code>: yearly awakening limit, free-license check, protected promises, and replacement selection. This also contains a fallback to a different outfield role when same-role cover is absent; that is a useful investigation point, not a proven full explanation of both lineup failures.</p><p><code>src/game/market.ts</code>: D5 scout current-strength ceiling calculation and role-overall filter. Jae’s OVR 86 falls below the current ceiling of 88.</p><p><code>src/game/pyramid.ts</code>: D1 support rating 103, star focus 212, keeper REF 180, typical PAC 112, and the shallow pace-curve note.</p><p><code>src/sim/tactics.ts</code> and <code>src/sim/engine.ts</code>: energy mode multipliers and condition scaling. The general non-STA scale bottoms at 75% of the action stat, before other context-specific effects.</p><p><code>src/game/coach-speech.ts</code>: a speech spends all held TP and supplies +10 attributes for D1’s second half. It was used in the cup final.</p><p><code>content/events.json</code>: the fire’s 30% save-all / 70% lose-primary branches. <code>MarketScreen.tsx</code>, <code>CoachStaffOverlay.tsx</code>, and <code>content/i18n/en.json</code> confirm the radio and copy findings.</p></details>
<details class="evidence-block"><summary>Important corrections to earlier assumptions</summary><ul><li>The report originally missed the existing outside-dismiss batch skip. Training behavior is unchanged; a cross-player plan is optional.</li><li>The fire did not demonstrably destroy the wrong building. The preview named a top shop <em>or</em> largest facility; the problem is ambiguity and framing.</li><li>Jae’s scout result was an extreme value find, not a demonstrated violation of the current implemented OVR ceiling.</li><li>The player’s SUPER percentage is a drill bonus chance, not an awakening probability.</li><li>Buying permit five did not establish that buying a permit always guarantees an awakening next match.</li><li>Tier 5 is not always more TP-efficient than Tier 4. The live cost/gain preview matters.</li><li>Quick Match and watched results were mixed. This run cannot prove their relative difficulty or parity.</li><li>One late collapse after Protect does not prove Protect is broken.</li><li>The browser crash does not prove an iOS memory or rendering defect.</li></ul></details>
<details class="evidence-block"><summary>What was not fully tested or recorded</summary><ul><li>Audio quality, music fatigue, haptics, native iPhone performance, and ordinary touch input.</li><li>Sustained MANUAL power timing. AUTO was used for the career.</li><li>All powers, every formation, all scouting briefs, all event branches, and every facility upgrade.</li><li>Penalty-shootout interaction and its skip flow.</li><li>D1 offseason growth. The run ended at the completed journey, before another season.</li><li>A reliable complete count of clicks, watched minutes, or modal dismissals.</li><li>The S1 cup play-in’s exact full-time score; one S3 Kindle result; one S5 Garnet result.</li><li>The S4 runner-up’s exact final points and the final S6 post-league cash balance.</li></ul></details>
<details class="evidence-block"><summary>What held up in this run</summary><ul><li>Save Energy survived the checked goals, powers, halftime transitions, and substitutions. No spontaneous reset was observed.</li><li>Saved career progress survived the renderer failures and reopening in a new tab.</li><li>The later scout reports used for signings matched the inspected players’ attributes.</li><li>Several formation and roster changes selected valid roles; the specific failures are listed rather than generalized to every change.</li><li>Rollover explained youth additions. Four S5 contract expiries did not trigger the earlier mass-departure fire in the next observed opening.</li><li>Pitch cards could be omitted in later negotiation rounds.</li><li>The ending recognized winning both D1 and the Hero Cup.</li></ul></details>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>My own play errors matter.</strong> I missed early scouting timing, some requests, and a few live tactical windows. I overvalued high PAC before checking its curve. I left useful late upgrades untested. These weaken claims about the fastest route, universal difficulty, and whether the economy lacks worthwhile spending.</p><p>The final result remains valid: both trophies were won through the game’s visible controls, with the allowed exploits, on the same career.</p></div><button id="print-report" class="screen-only">Print or save as PDF</button><a class="back-top" href="#top">Back to the full issue list</a></section></main>
<footer class="wrap footer"><p>Prepared from the Bramble Rovers playthrough. All proposed fixes are recommendations; none were implemented in the game. The HTML has no external libraries, fonts, trackers, or network dependencies.</p></footer>''')
